Present frames on ANGLE Vulkan without GPU timestamp queries.

GLSurfacePresentationHelper times each frame with a GL_TIMESTAMP query when
the context offers disjoint timer queries, and the system GLES driver does
not, so on GLES it uses a fence. ANGLE's Vulkan backend does offer them,
and implements each one as a one-off queue submission whose result
CheckPendingFrames then waits for: on a Mate 70 Pro+ the GPU thread spent
20-46 ms a frame there, and 1440p60 HDR video dropped 36% of its frames
on ANGLE Vulkan against none on GLES. On OHOS under ANGLE Vulkan the
helper goes to the fence path GLES already takes.

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-vulkan-present-fence.patch

diff --git a/ui/gl/gl_surface_presentation_helper.cc b/ui/gl/gl_surface_presentation_helper.cc
--- a/ui/gl/gl_surface_presentation_helper.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_surface_presentation_helper.cc
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include "ui/gl/egl_timestamps.h"
 #include "ui/gl/gl_context.h"
 #include "ui/gl/gl_fence.h"
+#include "ui/gl/gl_implementation.h"
 #include "ui/gl/gpu_timing.h"
 
 namespace gl {
@@ -212,6 +213,14 @@ void GLSurfacePresentationHelper::OnMakeCurrent(GLContext* context,
   gpu_timing_client_ = context->CreateGPUTimingClient();
   if (!gpu_timing_client_->IsAvailable())
     gpu_timing_client_ = nullptr;
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // ANGLE's Vulkan backend writes a timestamp query with a submission of its
+  // own and waits on the GPU to read it back: on a Mate 70 Pro+ that held the
+  // GPU thread 20-46 ms a frame and dropped a third of 1440p60 video. A fence
+  // says when the frame is done just as well, for nothing.
+  if (GetANGLEImplementation() == ANGLEImplementation::kVulkan)
+    gpu_timing_client_ = nullptr;
+#endif
 
 // https://crbug.com/854298 : disable GLFence on Android as they seem to cause
 // issues on some devices.
